Stop KernelSU writing policyload=0 into /sys/fs/selinux/status.

THIS PATCH APPLIES TO KernelSU'S TREE, NOT THE KERNEL TREE

Every other file in this directory is rooted at the kernel source
(security/selinux/...). This one is rooted at the KernelSU checkout, so
`a/kernel/selinux/rules.c` means $KSU_FOLDER/kernel/selinux/rules.c, NOT
<kernel>/kernel/selinux/rules.c -- which does not exist.
scripts/apply_nomount_stack.sh gets this right:

	apply_or_die "$HOOK/fix_selinux_seqno.patch" "$KSU_FOLDER"

Applying it by hand from the kernel root fails with "can't find file to patch",
and nothing in this file used to say why. It was also the only patch here with
no header, no applies-to matrix and no verification note at all.

THE ORACLE

`selinux_status_update_policyload(seqno)` does not INCREMENT anything -- it
ASSIGNS `status->policyload = seqno` in the mmap'd /sys/fs/selinux/status page
(bumping `status->sequence` either side so a concurrent reader can detect the
update). KernelSU calls reset_avc_cache() from its runtime rule path with
seqno == 0.

So on an unpatched kernel the observable is not "a higher load count". It is
`policyload == 0` -- a value the kernel only ever writes before the first policy
has loaded, and therefore impossible on a running device -- sitting next to a
`sequence` that has advanced past what a stock boot produces. Either half is a
tell; the pair together is unambiguous, and the status page is world-readable.

Dropping the two calls leaves both fields exactly as a stock boot leaves them.
Verified on OP15: policyload = 1, sequence matching one boot-time load.

WHAT IT COSTS, AND WHAT IT DELIBERATELY LEAVES

`avc_ss_reset()` is KEPT, so the KERNEL's own AVC still flushes and the new rules
take effect. Only userspace AVC caches (libselinux) miss the notification, so
they can hold a stale decision across a KernelSU policy change. Narrow in
practice: KernelSU installs its rules early in boot, before most userspace
caching matters.

`selnl_notify_policyload(0)` is also KEPT, and that is a decision, not an
oversight -- it broadcasts a policy-load event on NETLINK_SELINUX that no stock
device emits after boot. Receiving it needs a netlink_selinux_socket, and no app
domain is allowed to create one (measured 2026-08-25 against the live OP15
policy: untrusted_app, priv_app, isolated_app, platform_app and system_app all
compute 0 for class 35). Gating it would trade a tell no app can observe for a
documented bootloop risk. common/_hook/README.md records this as "Closed, will
not fix (was M-C2)", and both this file and the CI verifier assert the call
survives so it cannot be removed casually.

APPLIES TO

The `reset_avc_cache()` body is identical across the KernelSU forks the builder
fleet uses, even though its line number is not. Pre-image counted, then dry-run
at -F0:

	KernelSU-Next/KernelSU-Next @ next    1 occurrence   applies at -F0
	tiann/KernelSU              @ main    1 occurrence   applies at -F0
	ShirkNeko/SukiSU-Ultra      @ main    1 occurrence   applies at -F0

Both `selinux_status_update_policyload` call sites are removed -- the
`>= 6.4` arm and the `selinux_state` arm -- so the fork's own version gating is
untouched and only the two calls go.

TWO LINES OF CONTEXT, AND THAT IS FORCED

The line above the hunk is the fork-specific `#if`: KernelSU-Next splits it as

	#if ((!defined(KSU_COMPAT_USE_SELINUX_STATE)) || \
		LINUX_VERSION_CODE >= KERNEL_VERSION(6, 4, 0))

where tiann/KernelSU and SukiSU-Ultra put it on one line. So three lines of
leading context reaches out of the common region and the patch stops applying to
two of the three forks -- measured: regenerating this at -U3 made KernelSU and
SukiSU FAIL while KernelSU-Next kept working. Two lines on each side is the most
that is common, it is SYMMETRIC (which is what `patch -F0` cares about), and the
pre-image is unique in all three forks -- counted, not assumed.

What WAS wrong with the previous revision is everything around the hunk: no
header, no applies-to matrix, no note that the paths are relative to the KernelSU
checkout, and no `@@` function name. This is now generated the way every other
file here is -- by editing a real tree and running `diff -u2 -p` -- so the
function name is present and the hunk is correct by construction.

--- a/kernel/selinux/rules.c
+++ b/kernel/selinux/rules.c
@@ -63,10 +63,8 @@ static void reset_avc_cache()
     avc_ss_reset(0);
     selnl_notify_policyload(0);
-    selinux_status_update_policyload(0);
 #else
     struct selinux_avc *avc = selinux_state.avc;
     avc_ss_reset(avc, 0);
     selnl_notify_policyload(0);
-    selinux_status_update_policyload(&selinux_state, 0);
 #endif
     selinux_xfrm_notify_policyload();
